<section class="page-hero page-hero--compact">
  <p class="eyebrow">Setup guide</p>
  <h1>Verifying your organization domain.</h1>
  <p>You add one TXT record to your domain's DNS. We look it up, see it, and mark your organization verified. Nothing is installed and nothing changes about how your domain serves mail or web traffic.</p>
</section>

<section class="band band--white">
  <article class="callout callout--warning">
    <strong>The mistake almost everybody makes</strong>
    <span>
      Most DNS providers add your domain to the host field for you. If you type
      <code>_aegis-challenge.contoso.com</code> you will usually end up with
      <code>_aegis-challenge.contoso.com.contoso.com</code>, which resolves to nothing
      and looks exactly like "it did not work". Unless the guide below says
      otherwise, enter <strong>only</strong> <code>_aegis-challenge</code>.
    </span>
  </article>

  <div class="section-heading">
    <h2>What you are adding</h2>
    <p>Your organization's page in Aegis ID shows the exact value to use. It looks like this:</p>
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Type</th><td><code>TXT</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Name / Host</th><td><code>{{exampleHost}}</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Value</th><td><code class="wrap-anywhere">{{exampleValue}}</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>TTL</th><td>The lowest your provider allows while you are setting up</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <article class="blade-guidance">
    <strong>How long does it take?</strong>
    <span>
      Usually a minute or two, sometimes a few hours. The TTL on your existing records
      is what decides it, not the TTL you set on the new one. If the check fails the
      first time, wait and try again — the record does not need re-creating.
    </span>
  </article>
</section>

<section class="band band--light">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <h2>Step by step, by provider</h2>
    <p>Find yours below. If your provider is not listed, the record above is standard and any DNS host can add it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="provider-grid">
    {{#each providers}}
      <article class="provider-card" id="{{this.id}}">
        <h3>{{this.name}}</h3>
        <p class="table-note">
          The <strong>{{this.hostFieldName}}</strong> field expects
          {{#if (eq this.hostFieldExpects "label-only")}}the label only — <code>_aegis-challenge</code>{{else}}the full record name{{/if}}.
        </p>
        <ol class="provider-steps">
          {{#each this.steps}}<li>{{{this}}}</li>{{/each}}
        </ol>
        {{#if this.cli}}
          <p class="table-note">From the command line:</p>
          <pre class="provider-cli"><code>{{this.cli}}</code></pre>
        {{/if}}
      </article>
    {{/each}}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--white">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <h2>If the check keeps failing</h2>
  </div>
  <ul class="help-list">
    <li><strong>Check the full name.</strong> Look up <code>_aegis-challenge.yourdomain.com</code> yourself: <code>dig TXT _aegis-challenge.yourdomain.com +short</code>, or <code>nslookup -type=TXT _aegis-challenge.yourdomain.com</code> on Windows. If that returns nothing, the record is not published yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Check for a doubled domain.</strong> If <code>dig TXT _aegis-challenge.yourdomain.com.yourdomain.com</code> returns your value, the host field was over-filled.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the value is complete.</strong> Long values get truncated by a careless paste. It must match exactly, including the <code>aegis-domain-verification=</code> prefix.</li>
    <li><strong>Check you edited the live zone.</strong> If your nameservers point at another provider — Cloudflare, your host, your registrar — the record has to go wherever the nameservers point, not wherever you bought the domain.</li>
    <li><strong>Quotes.</strong> Most providers add them for you; AWS Route 53 does not. If you see quotes stored inside the value on a provider that adds its own, remove yours.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="table-note">Still stuck? The record expires after 14 days — start the claim again for a fresh one.</p>
</section>
